{
 "id": "violinist",
 "channel": "philosophy",
 "style": "ps2",
 "title": "THE VIOLINIST",
 "angle": "If someone needs your body to stay alive, are you obliged to let them use it?",
 "answer": "Thomson argued that even if a foetus is a person, a right to life is not a right to use another's body, and US law agrees for the living: a court refused to force a man to donate marrow to his dying cousin; critics reply that pregnancy usually follows a choice and that unplugging lets die while abortion kills, so the real disagreement is about what we owe with our bodies.",
 "logline": "You wake up plugged into a dying violinist, so must you stay? An MIT philosopher built the case in 1971 and expected most people to say you may unplug, but it was really about abortion: grant the foetus is a person, and a right to life still isn't a right to someone's body, but a Pittsburgh court faced it for real in 1978 and refused to force a marrow donation, so the dying man died, but critics answer that you were kidnapped and unplugging only lets die, so the question is what we owe each other's bodies.",
 "hook_text": "PLUGGED INTO A STRANGER",
 "hooks": [
  "The philosopher who named the trolley problem wrote a stranger puzzle in 1971.",
  "A court ruled a man did not have to give his dying cousin bone marrow."
 ],
 "world": {
  "scenery": "room"
 },
 "script": [
  {
   "text": "You wake up with a stranger's blood running through your kidneys.",
   "beat": "hook"
  },
  {
   "text": "Unplug him, and he dies; stay in bed nine months, and he lives.",
   "beat": "hook",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "So are you obliged to stay plugged in?",
   "beat": "question",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "An MIT philosopher, who later named the trolley problem, built this in 1971.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "answer",
   "say": "An M I T philosopher, who later named the trolley problem, built this in nineteen seventy-one."
  },
  {
   "text": "Music lovers kidnapped you: only your blood could save their famous violinist.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "Most people say you may unplug, though he has a right to life.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "But the violinist was never about music: the paper was about abortion.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "She even granted that a foetus is a person from conception.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "Even so, she argued, a right to life isn't a right to use someone else's body.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "But a court faced the same question for real, in Pittsburgh in 1978.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "but",
   "say": "But a court faced the same question for real, in Pittsburgh in nineteen seventy-eight."
  },
  {
   "text": "A man dying of anaemia needed bone marrow; only his cousin matched.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "The cousin refused; the judge called that morally indefensible.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "Still, the court wouldn't force him; the man died weeks later.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "But critics answer: you were kidnapped, while most pregnancies follow a choice.",
   "beat": "turn",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "Unplugging lets someone die, they add; abortion kills.",
   "beat": "turn",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "So people split less over who's a person than over what we owe with our bodies.",
   "beat": "payoff",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "Does a right to life include a right to someone else's body?",
   "beat": "payoff",
   "link": "so"
  }
 ],
